PQ(SPG, '2023T1q1', 47, 1, 'mcq', { fam: "g-commbarrier",
  cs: ["comm-process"],
  week: 2,
  diff: "hard",
  q: "<p>Which of the following can be classified as ‘information overload’ type barriers to communication?</p>",
  opts: ["The information received by a colleague at the workplace is very detailed.", "The information sent to an employee is related to concepts that are not familiar to the employee.", "If the information sender’s positive characteristics affect the receiver’s acceptance of a message.", "None of these"],
  ans: 1,
  why: ["Very detailed information is closer to the textbook meaning, but the key does not accept it.", "Key: information about unfamiliar concepts is treated as an overload barrier.", "False: the sender’s positive traits affecting acceptance is the halo effect, a perception barrier.", "False."],
  sol: "<p>The key treats information the receiver cannot place (unfamiliar concepts) as overload: more new material than the person can absorb → option 2.</p><p class=\"small muted\"><b>Note on the key:</b> The official key is option 2. In the usual definition, information overload means receiving more information than one can process (option 1 is closer). We follow the key and record the disagreement on the Sources page.</p>",
  trap: "Option 1 matches the everyday meaning of “overload”, which is why many pick it." });
