PQ(SPG, '2023T2', 90, 5, 'short', { fam: "g-sa",
  cs: ["comm-process"],
  week: 2,
  diff: "hard",
  q: "<p>If you learned that Jack dislikes female professionals, what additional insight does that provide regarding communication difficulties between him and Ms. Lee? What might you suggest to Jack? (Answer in 100-150 words)</p>",
  model: "<p>A bias against female professionals is a <b>psychological barrier</b> (prejudice, a preconceived notion) and a filter that distorts listening. It explains why Jack expects excuses, interrupts and dismisses her before hearing the message: <b>selective perception</b> makes him hear what he already believes.</p><p><b>Suggestions to Jack:</b> recognise the bias and how it affects his judgement (self-awareness); separate the person from the message by listening to the facts first; use active listening and summarise before responding; seek feedback from colleagues; and, if needed, take formal training. The organisation should also treat this as a fairness issue, not just a communication one.</p>",
  trap: "Treating prejudice as a personality clash and not naming the barrier.",
  derived: "Written answers have no official key; this model answer was written for the platform from the case and the course frameworks.",
  block: {"id": "SW", "text": "<p>Read the case study, then answer the given subquestions that follow in about 100-150 words each. Ensure your answers are supported with relevant content from the course and/or suitable examples.</p><p>Case: Southwestern Project: Communication Turmoil</p><p>Jack asked, “How is the Southwestern project coming along?” Ms. Lee said, “I need to talk to you about that.” “I don’t want to hear any excuses, just get this project done,” Jack yelled to Wei Lee. Ms. Lee said, “Fine. I quit. Do it yourself.” Ms. Lee is the senior project manager and is in charge of completing a customized training program for the firm’s largest client. Jack was hired from the outside just two months ago as vice president of training. Ms. Lee believes that her credentials are more appropriate and impressive than are Jack’s. Ms. Lee expected the job. She has no alternatives lined up. Jack has already earned a reputation for being harsh in a place where people formerly enjoyed working. The particular project in question includes extensive material/course manual preparation as well as classroom sessions. Before the interruption, Ms. Lee was attempting to communicate to Jack that the client had requested a significant variation from what was originally contracted. The good news that Ms. Lee had to share was that the client had agreed to double the fee.</p>"} });
