PQ(SPG, '2024T2', 91, 5, 'short', { fam: "g-sa",
  cs: ["nonverbal"],
  week: 2,
  diff: "moderate",
  q: "<p>In videoconferencing platforms like Zoom, learners in online or hybrid programs can easily withhold communication by turning off their microphones and video functions. Similarly, in organisations, like an elephant in the Zoom, employees might withhold information, ideas, or feedback (positive or negative) during virtual meetings. What drives this behaviour? Could there be any positive outcomes for teams or the organization from it? (Answer in 100 words)</p>",
  model: "<p><b>Drivers of withholding (employee silence):</b> fear of being judged or of retaliation, low psychological safety, a belief that speaking up changes nothing, power distance, and the ease of hiding behind a muted camera. These are psychological and organisational barriers.</p><p><b>Possible positives:</b> silence can give people time to reflect and send a better-thought-out written comment later; it can prevent an emotional reaction in the moment; and it can reduce information overload in large meetings. These benefits only hold when there is a safe channel to speak up later.</p>",
  trap: "Listing only negatives; the question also asks for possible positive outcomes.",
  derived: "Written answers have no official key; this model answer was written for the platform from the course frameworks." });
PQ(SPG, '2025T2', 230, 5, 'short', { fam: "g-sa",
  cs: ["comm-10cs"],
  week: 2,
  diff: "moderate",
  q: "<p>[Teaming Across Boundaries] The misunderstanding between Vikas and Rita about the use of certain words highlights the role of communication context, cultural differences, and communication skills in virtual teams. How could Vikas have communicated more clearly in asynchronous, cross-cultural communication? Answer in about 100 words. Guidelines: Support your answer with principles from business communication (e.g. 10Cs of Communication, context in communication or any other relevant theories/ frameworks).</p>",
  model: "<p>In asynchronous, cross-cultural messages there is no tone or face to repair a misreading, so Vikas should have used a <b>low-context</b> style: spell meaning out.</p><ul><li><b>Clear and concrete:</b> avoid ambiguous or idiomatic words; give dates, numbers and examples.</li><li><b>Complete:</b> state the request, the reason and the deadline in one message.</li><li><b>Considerate and courteous:</b> remember that a direct word in one culture may read as rude in another; add context and a polite frame.</li><li><b>Check understanding:</b> ask the reader to confirm or summarise.</li></ul>",
  trap: "Using the 10 Cs of teamwork instead of the 10 Cs of communication.",
  derived: "The case text was not included in the paper provided, so this model answer uses only what the question itself states and the course frameworks." });
