PQ(SPG, '2025T3', 218, 5, 'short', { fam: "g-sa",
  cs: ["comm-10cs"],
  week: 2,
  diff: "moderate",
  q: "<p>[When Every Word Matters: Communicating Layoffs] Which draft should Devika send? Justify your answer using any five of 10 Cs of communication or any other relevant business communication framework. (Answer in about 100 words, 5 marks)</p>",
  model: "<p>Devika should send the draft that is honest and human, judged on five of the 10 Cs:</p><ol><li><b>Clear:</b> states plainly that roles are being cut and why.</li><li><b>Complete:</b> includes what affected employees get (notice, severance, support) and what happens next.</li><li><b>Correct:</b> facts and numbers that will not need retracting.</li><li><b>Considerate:</b> acknowledges the impact on people before the business case.</li><li><b>Courteous:</b> respectful, no corporate euphemism or blame.</li></ol><p>A draft that hides behind jargon or spin fails these tests even if it is shorter.</p>",
  trap: "Picking a draft without testing it against named Cs.",
  derived: "The case text was not included in the paper provided, so this model answer uses only what the question itself states and the course frameworks. Because the two drafts are not reproduced, the answer gives the criteria that decide the choice." });
PQ(SPG, '2025T3', 220, 3, 'short', { fam: "g-sa",
  cs: ["comm-10cs"],
  week: 2,
  diff: "moderate",
  q: "<p>[When Every Word Matters: Communicating Layoffs] Suppose you read the CEO’s message and later learn it was fully drafted using generative AI tools.</p><p>Imagine you are a “retained employee” at SynapseAI. In the subsequent phase, explain your willingness to engage (e.g. share concerns, ask questions) versus withdraw (e.g. not offer suggestions, not share ideas) within the organization? (Answer in about 100 words, 3 marks)</p>",
  model: "<p>As a retained employee, learning the message was fully AI-written would lower my trust: the words of care would feel <b>inauthentic</b>, failing the “considerate” and “courteous” tests in substance even if they pass on the surface.</p><p>I would be more likely to <b>withdraw</b> — keep concerns to myself and avoid suggestions — unless leadership showed authenticity in other ways: open town halls, honest answers, and visible follow-through. If they did, I would re-engage and share concerns, because psychological safety comes from actions, not drafting tools.</p>",
  trap: "Giving only a yes/no; the question asks you to explain engagement versus withdrawal.",
  derived: "The case text was not included in the paper provided, so this model answer uses only what the question itself states and the course frameworks." });
PQ(SPG, '2025T3', 221, 3, 'short', { fam: "g-sa",
  cs: ["comm-10cs"],
  week: 2,
  diff: "moderate",
  q: "<p>[When Every Word Matters: Communicating Layoffs] Suppose you read the CEO’s message and later learn it was fully drafted using generative AI tools.</p><p>Imagine you are a “laid-off employee.” What would you want to hear in the CEO’s message? How would your reactions differ if the message were AI-generated versus written directly by the human leadership (CEO/HR team)? (Answer in about 100 words, 3 marks)</p>",
  model: "<p>As a laid-off employee I would want to hear: a clear reason for the decision, an acknowledgement that it is not my fault, concrete support (severance, notice, insurance, references, placement help), next steps with dates, and genuine thanks.</p><p>If I learned the message was AI-generated, I would feel the leaders had outsourced a human duty; the same words would feel cold and less credible. A message written by the CEO or HR — even if imperfect — signals respect and accountability, which matters most at a moment of loss.</p>",
  trap: "Answering only the first part.",
  derived: "The case text was not included in the paper provided, so this model answer uses only what the question itself states and the course frameworks." });
