PQ(SPG, '2024T3', 193, 6, 'short', { fam: "g-sa",
  cs: ["lead-self"],
  week: 4,
  diff: "moderate",
  q: "<p>An excellent way to become more self-aware about our proactivity is to evaluate where we focus our time and energy. Explain the concept of “spheres of responsibility” and discuss how it helps to lead yourself from within. (Answer in 150-200 words)</p><p>Guidelines: If you are a seasoned professional, provide examples from your professional life and explain how this concept applies or will help you in a leadership role. If you are a young learner, you may provide examples from personal or professional experiences, and describe how this approach can contribute to achieving a leadership role in the future.</p>",
  model: "<p><b>Spheres of responsibility</b> sort everything that occupies us into three circles:</p><ul><li><b>Sphere of control</b> — what is within my control: my thoughts, effort, skills, health, responses.</li><li><b>Sphere of influence</b> — outside my control but directly affecting me, where I can act through others: my team’s process, my manager’s view of my work.</li><li><b>Sphere of concern</b> — outside my control and indirect: the economy, a pandemic, policy changes.</li></ul><p><b>How it helps leading from within:</b> proactive people spend most energy in control, some in influence, and little in concern. Example: before placements, worrying about the job market (concern) changes nothing; practising interviews and building projects (control) and asking seniors for referrals (influence) do. A leader who models this focus keeps a team calm and productive in uncertainty.</p>",
  trap: "Defining the spheres without a personal example or without linking to proactivity.",
  derived: "Written answers have no official key; this model answer was written for the platform from the course framework." });
