PQ(SPG, '2024T3', 192, 7, 'short', { fam: "g-sa",
  cs: ["conflict-tki"],
  week: 5,
  diff: "hard",
  q: "<p>Review the case provided and consider the five possible solutions listed. Select the solution(s) you believe is/are the most effective approach for the given scenario. In your response, clearly explain why you think this solution is best suited, using relevant theories, frameworks, or best practices in the workplace dynamics. You must include concepts related to effective teamwork/ communication/ leadership/ emotional intelligence/ conflict management to support your reasoning.</p><p>[Case] Jalaj has recently been promoted to supervisor of his group. He got along well with everyone in the group and had a few close friends in the group, including Ciril. Six months into Jalaj’s tenure as supervisor, Ciril told him that Gayathri has started complaining that Jalaj was playing favourites and giving the best and most interesting assignments to his friends, leaving her and the other “outsiders” with the routine jobs that would not help them become more promotable. Gayathri apparently was threatening to go to Jalaj’s boss to complain about his unfair and arbitrary project assignments.</p><p>What should Jalaj do?</p><p>A. Ask Ciril to keep an eye on the situation and report to him about anything Gayathri says about his management style.</p><p>B. Speak to Gayathri directly, tell her what Ciril said, and ask her to explain herself.</p><p>C. Call a meeting of the entire group and ask for feedback on his management style, then, work to improve his relationships with all of his employees.</p><p>D. Make sure that Gayathri gets her choice of assignments when the next set of projects opens up.</p><p>E. Pre-empt any complaint by Gayathri by going to his supervisor now, explaining the situation and asking for advice on how to handle it.</p><p>Answer in (150-250 words) Guidelines: Choose the option(s) and justify your response with frameworks, theories, reasons or suitable examples.</p>",
  model: "<p><b>Best: C</b> (a group feedback meeting and improving relationships with everyone), supported by an honest, private conversation with Gayathri that does not reveal Ciril as the source.</p><ul><li><b>A</b> (Ciril as informer) destroys trust and confirms favouritism.</li><li><b>B</b> (confronting Gayathri with what Ciril said) breaks confidence and puts her on the defensive.</li><li><b>D</b> (giving her first choice) treats the symptom and is itself favouritism.</li><li><b>E</b> (pre-empting with the boss) is defensive and avoids the team.</li></ul><p>Option C is collaborating in TKI terms (high assertive, high cooperative). It shows EI (self-awareness about how a new supervisor’s friendships look; empathy for “outsiders”), and builds psychological safety. Jalaj should follow with a transparent, rotating assignment process so perceptions of fairness rest on evidence.</p>",
  trap: "Choosing an option without explaining why the others are worse, or without naming a framework.",
  derived: "Written answers have no official key; several options can be argued. This model answer argues C with supporting actions." });
