PQ(SPG, '2023T3', 198, 5, 'short', { fam: "g-sa",
  cs: ["cultural-dimensions"],
  week: 6,
  diff: "moderate",
  q: "<p>Read the case study, then answer the question in approximately 150-200 words each. Ensure that your responses are supported with pertinent content from the course, incorporating theories and/or suitable examples to demonstrate their application in practical scenarios within the context of the case study.</p><p>Caselet: MosaicTech Solutions</p><p>MosaicTech Solutions (MTS), a multinational technology company with a global footprint spanning the United States, India, Germany, and Japan, places a premium on diversity and encourages a collaborative ethos that transcends cultural boundaries. As the newly appointed senior project manager at MTS, the initial mandate is to helm a cross-functional team comprising engineers, designers, and marketers, each hailing from unique cultural backgrounds. This diverse ensemble is tasked with the ambitious goal of developing a cutting -edge mobile app designed to resonate with a worldwide audience.</p><p>Within the team, a tapestry of talent emerges — In the United States, Wayne, an Engineer, collaborates with Gordon, a Designer, and Selina, a Marketer. The Indian contingent includes Muthu (Engineer), Mathew (Designer), and Kamna (Marketer). Germany contributes Nicole (Engineer), Lena (Designer), and Markus (Marketer). Japan brings Hiroshi (Engineer), Yuki (Designer), and Akihiro (Marketer) to the project.</p><p>However, the team encounters challenges that underscore the richness of its diversity.Varied communication styles among team members become evident, ranging from those who prefer direct communication to those who favour indirect or context-dependent approaches. Compounding this, cultural norms significantly influence decision-making processes, with some cultures emphasizing consensus while others prioritize individual authority.</p><p>Against this backdrop, the project's overarching goals come into sharper focus: the development of an innovative mobile app that seamlessly caters to the diverse needs of a global user base and the simultaneous launch of the app across all target markets. This ambitious endeavour necessitates navigating the intricacies of cross-cultural collaboration, where effective communication and decision-making dynamics within the team become pivotal determinants of success.</p><p>[MosaicTech Solutions] Describe the communication style in the countries of team members. How can the senior project manager effectively address communication challenges within the cross-cultural team? (150-200 words) Hint: Consider the challenges, barriers and cultural differences in communication styles.</p>",
  model: "<p><b>Communication styles:</b> the USA and Germany are <b>low-context</b> (direct, explicit, written; Germans especially direct in negative feedback). Japan is strongly <b>high-context</b> (indirect, implicit, read the air). India is <b>high-context</b> and relationship-oriented, often indirect with seniors.</p><p><b>Challenges:</b> direct members may seem rude to Japanese and Indian colleagues; indirect “yes” may be misread as agreement by Americans and Germans; decision styles differ (consensus in Japan, top-down in India, individual authority in the US).</p><p><b>Actions for the manager:</b> agree team norms (explicit written summaries after every meeting; decisions and owners recorded); explain the context differences openly; create safe channels (one-to-ones, anonymous input) for indirect communicators; check understanding by asking for summaries rather than “any questions?”; rotate meeting times across time zones.</p>",
  trap: "Describing countries without proposing what the manager should do.",
  derived: "Written answers have no official key; this model answer was written for the platform from the case printed in the paper and the course frameworks." });
